ष्णभावनामृत\nआन्दोलन के कार्यकलापों में गुरुकुल का अत्यधिक महत्त्व है, क्योंकि गुरुकुल के बालकों को बचपन\nसे ही कृष्णभावनामृत के विषय में शिक्षा दी जाती है। वे अपने अन्तस्तल में स्थिर हो जाते हैं और जब\nवे बड़े होते हैं, तो प्रकृति के गुण उन्हें जीत नहीं पाते।\n\nइस प्रकार श्रीमद्भागवत के सप्तम स्कन्ध के अन्तर्गत “हिरण्यकशिपु का साधु पुत्र प्रह्लाद",
      "textEn": "205\n\nThose who have abandoned all playful games due to that greatness. Bala, unclean, through the two doors, the eye, the ear, and the tongue. 56. Prayers offered by the king, with a mind full of devotion. Taanah Karuno Maitro Mahabhagavato Asurah. 57 Meaning of the word\n\nTe—they; Tu—indeed; Tat-gauravat—with great respect for the words of Prahlad Maharaj (because he was his devotee); Sarve—all; Tyakta—renounced; Krida-parichhadaah—playing toys; Balah—boys; Adukshita-dhiyah—whose intellect is not corrupted like their\nfathers; Dwandva—in a sense of duality; Aram—one who enjoys (like a teacher like Shanda and Amarka);\nIrit—through teachings; Ihitah—through actions; Paryupasata—sat around; Raj-Indra—Oh King Yudhishthira; Tat—that;\nNyasta—renounced; Hriday-Ikshanaah—his heart and Eye; Tan—them; Aah—spoke; Karunah—extremely compassionate; Maitrah—\nA true friend; Maha-Bhagavatah—extremely venerable devotee; Asurah—though born from a demon father, Prahlada Maharaj I.\nHey King Yudhishthir, all the children loved Prahlad Maharaj very much and respected him.\nThey used to honor him. Due to his short life, he was not as tainted by the teachings and actions of his teachers,\nwhile his teachers were attached to slander, duality, and physical comfort. In this way,\nAll the children, leaving their toys, sat around Prahlad Maharaj to listen to his words. Their hearts and eyes were fixed on them, and they were looking at them enthusiastically. Prahlad\nMaharaj, although born in the demon clan, was a great devotee and he wished for the welfare of the demons. In this way, he began to preach to those children about the futility of a materialistic life. Meaning: Bala Adushit-Dhiyaha Pada indicates that the children were not as corrupted by materialism as their fathers due to their short lives.\nTherefore, Prahlad Maharaj, taking advantage of the ignorance of his classmates, began to teach them the importance of spiritual life and the futility of materialistic life. Although Shand and Amark were preaching materialistic life to all the children regarding religion, wealth, and desire,\nthe children were not more corrupted. Therefore, they\nwere eager to hear about Krishna Bhava Namrata from Prahlad Maharaj. In our Krishna consciousness movement,\nthe Gurukul has great importance in the activities of the movement, because the children of the Gurukul are educated about Krishna consciousness from childhood. They become stable in their inner selves, and when\nthey grow up, the qualities of nature cannot conquer them. In this way, under the seventh chapter of the Srimad Bhagavata, \"Hiranyakashipu's virtuous son Prahlada\""
